WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.860
Secure reviewers trace untrusted data from entry points to security-relevant use.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:17.320
Entry points may include user input, API input, file uploads, headers, cookies, webhooks, message queues, third-party responses, environment variables, and configuration files.

3
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:25.980
Not every input is equally risky, but every input has a source, an expected shape, and a level of trust that should be clear in the code.

4
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.940
Sensitive sinks are places where data has security impact.

5
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:39.960
Database queries, shell commands, template rendering, file paths, logs, outbound requests, and authorization decisions are common examples.

6
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:49.420
A review should ask what data reaches these sinks, what validation or transformation happened first, and whether the sink is used through safe APIs.

7
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.160
The question is not only whether a value exists, but whether the code uses it in a way that changes security behavior.

8
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:01.960
Trust boundaries show where data, identity, permissions, or assumptions change.

9
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:07.180
A browser request crossing into a server-side API crosses a boundary.

10
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.000
A webhook from a third party crosses another.

11
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:16.180
A background worker reading from a queue may receive data that was trusted by one component but not by another.

12
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:23.480
A reviewer should be cautious when code treats data as safe merely because it came from an internal service or configuration source.

13
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.040
Data-flow review also helps find missing controls.

14
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:38.180
If a value enters through an API and eventually becomes a file path, the reviewer can look for path normalization, allowed directories, extension handling, and error behavior.

15
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.200
If a parameter reaches an outbound HTTP request, the reviewer can ask whether the destination is constrained.

16
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:50.460
The goal is to understand how untrusted or sensitive data moves before deciding whether the implementation is safe.
